Sure! Here's an example of how a table of contents (TOC) can be generated automatically in a LaTeX document using the `\tableofcontents` command:

1. Begin your LaTeX document with the standard preamble, including the following packages:

   ```
   \documentclass{article}
   \usepackage{tocloft}
   \usepackage{lipsum} % for generating dummy text
   ```

   The `tocloft` package provides additional customization options for the table of contents, and the `lipsum` package is used to generate dummy text for this example.

2. Create the sections and subsections in your document using standard LaTeX commands:

   ```
   \begin{document}

   \section{First Section}
   \lipsum[1]

   \subsection{First Subsection}
   \lipsum[2]

   \subsection{Second Subsection}
   \lipsum[3]

   \section{Second Section}
   \lipsum[4]

   \subsection{First Subsection}
   \lipsum[5]

   \subsection{Second Subsection}
   \lipsum[6]

   \end{document}
   ```

   The `lipsum` package is used to generate dummy text for each section and subsection.

3. Add the `\tableofcontents` command after the `\begin{document}` command:

   ```
   \begin{document}

   \tableofcontents

   \section{First Section}
   \lipsum[1]

   \subsection{First Subsection}
   \lipsum[2]

   \subsection{Second Subsection}
   \lipsum[3]

   \section{Second Section}
   \lipsum[4]

   \subsection{First Subsection}
   \lipsum[5]

   \subsection{Second Subsection}
   \lipsum[6]

   \end{document}
   ```

   This will generate the TOC automatically based on the section and subsection headings in your document.

4. If you want to customize the appearance of the TOC, you can use the following options provided by the `tocloft` package:

   ```
   \usepackage{tocloft}

   \renewcommand{\cftsecfont}{\normalfont} % font for section titles
   \renewcommand{\cftsubsecfont}{\normalfont} % font for subsection titles
   \renewcommand{\cftsecleader}{\cftdotfill{\cftsecdotsep}} % leader for section numbers
   \renewcommand{\cftsubsecleader}{\cftdotfill{\cftsubsecdotsep}} % leader for subsection numbers
   \setlength{\cftsecnumwidth}{2em} % width of section numbers
   \setlength{\cftsubsecnumwidth}{3em} % width of subsection numbers

   \begin{document}

   \tableofcontents

   \section{First Section}
   \lipsum[1]

   \subsection{First Subsection}
   \lipsum[2]

   \subsection{Second Subsection}
   \lipsum[3]

   \section{Second Section}
   \lipsum[4]

   \subsection{First Subsection}
   \lipsum[5]

   \subsection{Second Subsection}
   \lipsum[6]

   \end{document}
   ```

   These commands change the font, leader, and width of section and subsection numbers in the TOC.

This should generate a TOC for your document automatically based on the section and subsection headings. You can adjust the customization options to fit your document's style.

To draw a chessboard in LaTeX, you can use the `chessboard` package. Here's an example code that includes all the required commands and packages to display a chessboard with the given FEN notation:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{chessboard}

\begin{document}

% Define the FEN notation
\newchessgame{game}{fen="rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"}

% Display the chessboard
\makepieceboard{game}

\end{document}
```

To customize the appearance of the chessboard, you can modify the options in `\newchessgame{}`. For example, if you want to use a different color scheme, you can use the `chessboard-style` option:

```latex
\newchessgame{game}{style="light", fen="rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"}
```

This will use a light color scheme for the board.

You can also use the `chessboard-size` option to change the size of the board:

```latex
\newchessgame{game}{style="dark", size="small", fen="rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1"}
```

This will use a dark color scheme for a small-sized board.

For more options and customization, refer to the `chessboard` package documentation.

